    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change lands in the right place and reads as a natural extension of the existing code:\n\n- `OwnerTelephone` is already the documented \"single source of truth for the owner-telephone E.164 rule,\" so adding the country-specific length check inside `normalize(...)` keeps all telephone-shaping logic in one cohesive unit rather than scattering it into the caller functions (`ValidateOwnerFields`, `CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique`). Both callers inherit the rule for free, which is exactly the point of this class.\n- The new rule is expressed as data (`NATIONAL_LENGTH` map) rather than a chain of `if`/`else`, which makes adding a third country code a one-line edit \u2014 appropriately additive and extensible for a rule that is inherently a lookup table.\n- The method stays small and single-purpose; nothing is duplicated, and the class is not turning into a catch-all. The class-level Javadoc and the map's own comment were both updated to explain the new behavior and the insertion-order rationale, matching the surrounding documentation-heavy style.\n- Returning `null` on a wrong-length national number reuses the existing \"invalid telephone \u2192 null \u2192 400\" convention already established for the generic 8\u201315 digit bound, so the new branch is consistent with how the method already signals rejection.\n\nNo structural changes needed.",
